################################################################################
# Trpiboot DRaspberry Pi 5 specifics

	This loader is started directly by the Raspberry Pi 5 VPU firmware,
	with no EFI and no EDK2 in the boot path.  The device tree comes from
	the firmware as a physical address in x0; there is no EFI memory map,
	so all memory information comes from the tree's /memory node.

	The console is the SoC PL011 at 0x107d001000, which the firmware has
	already configured to 115200 8N1 before entry.

	md0: is a memory disk embedded in the loader image itself, so it is
	available with no storage driver at all.  disk0: is the SD card.

	The loader boots from md0: unless loader.env on the card's FAT
	partition (disk0s1:) sets rootdev, for example "rootdev=disk0s2a:".
	It then reads /boot from that filesystem.  A boot started from
	tryboot.txt reads tryboot.env first.

################################################################################
# Ttryboot DReboot once using tryboot.txt

	tryboot [-n]

	Asks the VPU firmware, through its property mailbox, to read
	tryboot.txt instead of config.txt for the next boot only, then
	resets.  The flag is consumed by that boot, so every later boot is
	back on config.txt: a tryboot.txt that fails to boot costs one power
	cycle.  The flag is read back before resetting.

	-n	arm the flag without resetting.

################################################################################
# Tuntryboot DClear an armed TryBoot flag

	untryboot

################################################################################
# Tvcinfo DShow VPU firmware, board and TryBoot state

	vcinfo

	Firmware revision, board revision and serial, SoC temperature (there
	is no fan control at this prompt), the reboot flags that
	will apply to the next boot, and which config file this boot used
	(from /chosen/bootloader/tryboot in the firmware's device tree).

################################################################################
# Tmmu DShow the loader's MMU, cache and memory map

	mmu

	Whether the MMU and caches are on, the EL2 translation registers,
	the /memory ranges from the device tree, and the map as runs of one
	attribute: RAM write-back, the framebuffer non-cacheable, the rest
	Device-nGnRnE.  The kernel is entered with the MMU and D-cache off.

################################################################################
# Tsdinfo DShow the SD card and its controller

	sdinfo

	The loader reads the SD card in the board's slot as disk0:, polled
	and read-only, through the BCM2712's own SDHCI.  sdinfo prints the
	controller's state, the card's identity and size, and what the reads
	so far have cost.  Without a card it says why there is no disk0.

################################################################################
# Tusbinfo DShow the USB keyboard controllers and ports

	usbinfo

	The loader drives a USB keyboard for the HDMI console through RP1's
	two xHCI controllers, polled, with HID boot-protocol keyboards
	directly on a root port (no hubs).  RP1 is reachable only when the
	firmware leaves PCIe2 trained: config.txt pciex4_reset=0.  usbinfo
	prints why there is no keyboard, or each controller and what is on
	each connected port.

################################################################################
